1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:03,730
A device resets, and its battery
can still be most of the way full.

2
00:00:03,960 --> 00:00:06,410
If the charge did not
run out, then what did?

3
00:00:07,460 --> 00:00:08,850
Here is what is inside.

4
00:00:08,980 --> 00:00:12,790
A coin cell is a 3 volt source
with a small resistor hidden in it.

5
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:15,450
Energizer says a fresh
one is near 10 ohms.

6
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:17,430
When the device asks
for a burst of current,

7
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:20,110
some voltage is lost pushing
it through that resistor.

8
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:22,370
The bigger the burst, the more you lose.

9
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:26,450
I'm Tanmay Kulkarni, in
for Humanitarians AI.

10
00:00:26,820 --> 00:00:28,350
I did not open a real battery.

11
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:32,510
I built a model of a coin cell from
two manufacturers' published graphs,

12
00:00:32,530 --> 00:00:33,770
and I ask it one thing:

13
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:35,910
how much is left when the device resets?

14
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:41,530
Every device has a reset line, a voltage
it cannot go under, and I chose 2 volts.

15
00:00:41,680 --> 00:00:44,490
That gives the cell a limit,
the biggest burst it can pass.

16
00:00:44,780 --> 00:00:46,790
Fresh, that is about 100 milliamps.

17
00:00:46,920 --> 00:00:50,730
Remember this picture: the tank is the
charge left, and the line is that limit.

18
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:53,930
Now use the cell, and the resistor grows.

19
00:00:54,360 --> 00:00:57,550
Energizer's plot shows it
going from 10 ohms to about 65.

20
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:00,770
So the line comes down:
100 milliamps at the start,

21
00:01:00,880 --> 00:01:05,010
about 43 once nearly three quarters
is spent, about 8.5 at the end.

22
00:01:05,380 --> 00:01:08,270
The tank empties slowly,
but the line falls faster.

23
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:10,670
Now let's make two asks.

24
00:01:10,780 --> 00:01:14,210
8 milliamps never meets the line,
so the device uses the whole cell.

25
00:01:14,760 --> 00:01:17,570
60 milliamps meets it with the
tank still nearly half full,

26
00:01:17,590 --> 00:01:20,090
and the device resets with 48% left.

27
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:22,110
The same cell, and the same tank.

28
00:01:22,130 --> 00:01:25,270
What changed is only how big
a burst the device asked for.

29
00:01:26,420 --> 00:01:28,010
Let me be plain about what this is.

30
00:01:28,140 --> 00:01:31,630
It is a model fitted to graphs I
read, and I tested no real cell.

31
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:34,690
Cold would make it worse, and
I did not model a capacitor.

32
00:01:34,710 --> 00:01:38,250
And my reset line is my own
choice, so the shape is the claim,

33
00:01:38,270 --> 00:01:39,410
not the exact numbers.

34
00:01:40,540 --> 00:01:43,370
So when something resets with a
battery that still looks fine,

35
00:01:43,390 --> 00:01:45,930
ask how big its burst
is, not how much is left.

36
00:01:46,180 --> 00:01:49,270
A cell can die rich, and how
rich depends on what you ask.

37
00:01:49,290 --> 00:01:52,470
The long film adds a third ask,
and the sum you can do yourself.

38
00:01:52,660 --> 00:01:54,310
It is called The Cell Dies Rich.
